---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.4 — Comparing Objects &amp; The CommandParser Battle Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topic 2.6 · 90-Minute Design-First Build.</strong> Today you will design and build
    the command parser and combat engine for a multi-enemy dungeon encounter in
    <code>CommandParser.java</code>. Your Hero starts with <strong>60 HP, 25 Mana, and 2
    Potions</strong> facing an array of three enemies: a <code>goblin</code> (20 HP), a
    <code>skeleton</code> (25 HP), and an armored <code>boss</code> (45 HP).
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Design-First Workflow (One-Shot Challenge):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/CommandParser.java" download>CommandParser.java</a> — starter stub
            containing the <code>Enemy</code> class, Hero stats, <code>Enemy[] enemies</code>,
            provided roster helpers (<code>findEnemy</code> and <code>firstLivingEnemy</code>),
            an interactive battle loop in <code>main()</code>, and stubs for your 5 methods.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">
        <strong>How to work today:</strong>
    </p>
    <ol style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
        <li><strong>Design before coding:</strong> Answer the Guided Design questions for each part
            to avoid String <code>==</code> traps, null crashes, <code>indexOf(" ") == -1</code>
            crashes, and <code>compareTo == -1</code> bugs.</li>
        <li><strong>Draft &amp; battle in the sandbox:</strong> Copy each method into
            <code>CommandParser.java</code> and fight the encounter live in your terminal:</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>javac CommandParser.java
java CommandParser</code></pre>
    <p style="margin-top:0.4rem; margin-bottom:0;">
        <strong>Step 3 (One-Shot Pass):</strong> Once you can play a full battle cleanly (testing
        <code>attack</code>, <code>cast</code>, <code>drink potion</code>, <code>defend</code>, and
        invalid commands), unlock <code>CommandParserCheck.java</code> below and aim to pass all
        <strong>38 checks</strong> on your very first run.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Code Reading · The Encounter World</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~5 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Read First: How Player Stats &amp; the <code>Enemy[]</code> Roster Work</h3>

    <p>
        Open <code>CommandParser.java</code> and read the top half of the file before writing code:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong><code>Enemy</code> Fields:</strong> <code>e.name</code>, <code>e.hp</code>,
            <code>e.maxHp</code>, <code>e.attackDamage</code>, and <code>e.armored</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Hero State Variables:</strong> <code>playerHp = 60</code>,
            <code>maxHp = 60</code>, <code>mana = 25</code>, <code>potions = 2</code>, and
            <code>isShielded = false</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Provided Roster Helpers:</strong> <code>findEnemy(roster, targetName)</code>
            calls your <code>isSameCommand</code>, and <code>firstLivingEnemy(roster)</code> calls
            your <code>firstInTurnOrder</code>.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · Reference vs. Value Equality</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>isSameCommand(String input, String target)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Primitives compare raw values with <code>==</code>, whereas <code>String</code>
            variables hold memory references. When a player types <code>"attack goblin"</code>,
            <code>extractTarget</code> creates a brand-new heap <code>String</code> via
            <code>substring</code>. Using <code>==</code> would say that new <code>"goblin"</code>
            does not match the <code>"goblin"</code> literal inside <code>enemies</code>!
        </p>
        <h4>Specification</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li>Returns <code>true</code> iff both <code>input</code> and <code>target</code> are
                non-null and contain identical characters (case-sensitive); <code>false</code>
                otherwise.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>isSameCommand</code></h3>
        <p>Reason through reference equality and null safety:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong><code>==</code> vs. <code>.equals</code> &amp; Short-Circuit Null Guard:</strong>
                Why can <code>"attack" == "attack"</code> evaluate to <code>true</code> while
                <code>"attack goblin".substring(7) == "goblin"</code> evaluates to
                <code>false</code>? And why must <code>input != null &amp;&amp; target != null</code>
                come <em>before</em> <code>input.equals(target)</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>CommandParser.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your <code>isSameCommand</code> method body and copy it into
                <code>CommandParser.java</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 2 · Substring &amp; indexOf Parsing</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>extractVerb(String rawCommand)</code> &amp; <code>extractTarget(String rawCommand)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What These Functions Do &amp; Why They Exist</h4>
        <p>
            Splits user input at the first space (<code>" "</code>) into an action verb
            (<code>"attack"</code>, <code>"cast"</code>, or single-word <code>"defend"</code>) and
            an optional target (<code>"goblin"</code>, <code>"fireball"</code>, or <code>""</code>
            when there is no space).
        </p>
        <h4>Specification</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li>If <code>rawCommand == null || rawCommand.length() == 0</code>, both return <code>""</code>.</li>
            <li>If <code>int space = rawCommand.indexOf(" ");</code> is <code>&gt;= 0</code>:
                <code>extractVerb</code> returns <code>substring(0, space)</code>;
                <code>extractTarget</code> returns the substring strictly after the space.</li>
            <li>If <code>space == -1</code>: <code>extractVerb</code> returns <code>rawCommand</code>;
                <code>extractTarget</code> returns <code>""</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>extractVerb</code> &amp; <code>extractTarget</code></h3>
        <p>Catch the two classic parsing bugs before coding:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Missing Space &amp; Off-by-One Space Index:</strong> What does
                <code>rawCommand.indexOf(" ")</code> return on a single-word command like
                <code>"defend"</code>, and what crashes if you pass that to
                <code>substring</code> without checking <code>space == -1</code>? Also, in
                <code>"attack goblin"</code>, why does <code>rawCommand.substring(space)</code> fail
                to match <code>"goblin"</code>, and what starting index fixes it?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>CommandParser.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write both <code>extractVerb</code> and <code>extractTarget</code>, copy them into
                <code>CommandParser.java</code>, and test typing <code>attack goblin</code> in
                <code>java CommandParser</code> to inspect the <code>[Trace]</code> line?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Dictionary Ordering with compareTo</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>firstInTurnOrder(String nameA, String nameB)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Powers <code>firstLivingEnemy(roster)</code> so spells and counterattacks target living
            enemies in alphabetical turn order using <code>a.compareTo(b)</code> (negative when
            <code>a</code> comes first, <code>0</code> when equal, positive when <code>a</code>
            comes after).
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>firstInTurnOrder</code></h3>
        <p>Avoid the classic <code>compareTo</code> myth:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong><code>compareTo</code> Sign vs. <code>-1</code> &amp; Case Order:</strong>
                What integer does <code>"goblin".compareTo("skeleton")</code> return, and why does
                testing <code>compareTo(...) == -1</code> fail instead of <code>&lt;= 0</code>? Also,
                between <code>"goblin"</code> and <code>"Boss"</code>, which comes first in Unicode?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>CommandParser.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your <code>firstInTurnOrder</code> method body and copy it into
                <code>CommandParser.java</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · AP Synthesis: Full Combat Turn Resolver</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>resolveCombatTurn(String rawCommand, Enemy[] roster)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>Combat Action Specification Table</h4>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Verb</th><th>Target &amp; Preconditions</th><th>Effect on Hero &amp; <code>Enemy[] roster</code></th><th>Return</th></tr>
            <tr><td><code>null</code> / <code>""</code></td><td>Command null or verb <code>""</code></td><td>No changes</td><td><code>-1</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>"attack"</code></td><td><code>Enemy e = findEnemy(roster, target);</code> (<code>e == null</code> &rarr; <code>-1</code>)</td><td>Unarmored: 12; Armored+Shielded: 15; Armored+Unshielded: 6. Clamp <code>e.hp &gt;= 0</code>.</td><td>damage or <code>-1</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>"cast"</code></td><td>Target <code>""</code></td><td>No changes</td><td><code>-1</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>"cast"</code></td><td><code>"heal"</code> (5 mana)</td><td>If mana &lt; 5 fizzle (0); else <code>mana -= 5</code>, <code>playerHp += 20</code> (cap <code>maxHp</code>).</td><td><code>20</code> or <code>0</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>"cast"</code></td><td><code>"fireball"</code> (10 mana, hits <code>firstLivingEnemy(roster)</code>)</td><td>If <code>mana &lt; 10 || t == null</code> fizzle (0); else <code>mana -= 10</code>, <code>t.hp -= 25</code> (clamp 0).</td><td><code>25</code> or <code>0</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>"cast"</code></td><td>Other spell with <code>target.compareTo("m") &lt; 0</code> (8 mana)</td><td>If <code>mana &lt; 8 || t == null</code> fizzle (0); else <code>mana -= 8</code>, <code>t.hp -= 18</code> (clamp 0).</td><td><code>18</code> or <code>0</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>"cast"</code></td><td>Other spell with <code>target.compareTo("m") &gt;= 0</code> (12 mana)</td><td>If <code>mana &lt; 12 || t == null</code> fizzle (0); else <code>mana -= 12</code>, <code>t.hp -= 22</code> (clamp 0).</td><td><code>22</code> or <code>0</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>"drink"</code></td><td>Target must be <code>"potion"</code></td><td>Bad target &rarr; -1; <code>potions &lt;= 0</code> &rarr; 0; else <code>potions--</code>, <code>playerHp += 25</code> (cap), <code>mana += 10</code>.</td><td><code>25</code> / <code>0</code> / <code>-1</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>"defend"</code></td><td>Target must be <code>""</code></td><td><code>isShielded = true; mana += 5;</code></td><td><code>5</code> or <code>-1</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>"flee"</code></td><td>Target must be <code>""</code></td><td>Flee encounter</td><td><code>99</code> or <code>-1</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Other verb</td><td>Unknown command</td><td>No changes</td><td><code>-1</code></td></tr>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>resolveCombatTurn</code></h3>
        <p>Plan branch ordering inside <code>"cast"</code> before writing code:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Spell Branch Priority:</strong> Inside <code>"cast"</code>, why must you check
                specific spells (<code>"fireball"</code> and <code>"heal"</code>) <em>before</em>
                checking <code>target.compareTo("m") &lt; 0</code>? What would happen to
                <code>"cast fireball"</code> and <code>"cast heal"</code> if
                <code>compareTo("m")</code> came first?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Play in <code>CommandParser.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your <code>resolveCombatTurn</code> implementation (or outline its helper
                calls and branches), copy it into <code>CommandParser.java</code>, and play through
                a full fight in <code>java CommandParser</code> to defeat the <code>goblin</code>,
                <code>skeleton</code>, and <code>boss</code> before unlocking
                <code>CommandParserCheck.java</code> below?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>You played through and won an encounter in <code>java CommandParser</code></li>
            <li>You unlock <code>CommandParserCheck.java</code> below and aim for <strong><code>38 passed, 0 failed</code></strong> on your first run</li>
            <li>You have downloaded your <code>.json</code> answers file for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/comparing-objects.html">Comparing Objects</a> — equals, null safety, compareTo</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/string.html">String Methods</a> — indexOf, substring, length</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a> — exact String methods on the AP exam</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
---
